WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.801
-Welcome back to the show.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:06.033
-I love having you on.
I want to talk about everything.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:08.000
I don't even know
really where to start

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.133
because we have
this new book here

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.300
that is kind of not
for children, really.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.567
-No, it's -- it's my --
Well, it's not my book, even.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:17.200
-No, it's not.
-It's the pigeon's book.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.133
I'm just a translator.
-Ah!

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.534
So the pigeon is what --
is just fed up. Finally.

00:00:22.534 --> 00:00:25.534
-Pigeon just discovered
that he works in books.

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:27.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.334
-And said, "Are you kidding me?"

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:30.133
-And he's not happy
about the ending.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.300
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.701
He never gets
what he wants in the books.

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:35.167
-No.
-And you are laughing at him.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yes. And he does not --

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.400
-He's not happy about that,
either.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.801
-He wants to be respected.
-Right.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:45.567
And like all New York City
pigeons, he's a philosopher.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.467
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, he is.

00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:52.200
So, this is his lost
and profound wisdom.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.167
-Yeah.
-The peckings of a birdbrain.

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:58.133
-This one I love. It says,
"Success is 99% perspiration

00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:01.200
and 5% approximation."

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.234
Did you know that the pigeon
was gonna take off

00:01:06.234 --> 00:01:07.934
when you first did this?

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.501
-No, I didn't.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:14.334
Basically, 'cause everybody
told me it wouldn't.

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:17.367
So I create -- I do a little
sketchbook every year

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:19.100
for my clients and friends.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.601
I've done it for 30 years.
And one year, I did one.

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:24.767
It was called "Don't Let
the Pigeon Drive the Bus."

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:27.067
And I thought it was a funny,
weird little thing.

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:30.567
And my wife at the time --
who's still my wife.

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:32.267
-Wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.901
Okay. Wow.
-Happily.

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:37.000
-Okay. I was like,
"Who did I just --

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Who did I say hello to?"
Like, wow.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.267
That could've been rough.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.734
That could've been rough.
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:45.501
-Yet at the time...
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.367
-...was working as
a lower-school librarian,

00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:48.968
and she brought the sketchbook.

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:51.367
I actually brought a copy.
This is the original.

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.767
It's a little sketchbook I did
for clients and friends,

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.734
and she brought this
and read it to the kids

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:01.334
and said, "I think
this is a children's book."

00:02:01.334 --> 00:02:03.434
And I said, "Oh, you don't know
what you're talking about."

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:05.033
-Yeah.
-"No, that's obviously it's not.

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:06.334
It's just a little sketchbook.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:08.167
It's a little thing
for friends and whatever."

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:09.167
Yeah, that's it.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.801
-And this turned into...

00:02:10.801 --> 00:02:12.934
-Well, so what I did was,
I went to every publisher

00:02:12.934 --> 00:02:16.901
in New York, and they said,
"We agree with you.

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:18.434
It isn't a book.
Your wife is wrong."

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:20.934
[ Laughter ]
-Really, this is not --

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:24.067
-They said one word.
It's unusual.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:26.701
And I finally found a publisher

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:31.100
that said,
"Unusual is not pejorative."

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.601
-Wow.
-And then they published it.

00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:33.901
-Wow!
-So there you go.

00:02:33.901 --> 00:02:36.567
And that was 20 years ago.
20 years ago.

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:38.300
-I mean, is that wild?
-Yeah.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's amazing.

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:41.601
To look at what you've done.
Because now you --

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:44.334
-And that's for you.
That's my gift for you.

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:45.901
Yeah.
-An original?

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:47.400
-Yeah, that's it. Yeah.
-Are you kidding me?

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.267
-Absolutely.
No, I'm not kidding you.

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.033
I'm absolutely not kidding you.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:53.834
-I -- I -- I -- I, you know,
I'm the biggest fan of yours.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:55.033
This means a lot to me.

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.200
You are a hero in our house,
by the way.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.567
We read every one
of "Elephant and Piggie" books.

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:01.467
We have everything,
and we read them.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:02.601
I love your books.

00:03:02.601 --> 00:03:04.601
I want to read them
without the kids.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.467
-Great. Well, if you read them,
what you really should do,

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.434
if you love your kids,
every time,

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:12.734
it should be a fresh copy.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.667
-See? It's genius.
Genius, as well, yeah.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:19.567
-Well, I love that intimacy.
I mean, like, I love the idea

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:23.367
of parents and kids
doing this together, you know?

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:25.467
So part of what I want
to do in my books is,

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:27.667
I want my books
not to just be books.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:31.200
I want them to be sparks.
I want my books to be played.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.868
So that's why every character
is easy to draw.

00:03:33.868 --> 00:03:36.467
But I also want
for the grown-ups,

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:39.367
I want to give them
a shame-ectomy.

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:41.300
-A shame-ectomy.
-Yeah.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.734
Because here's your chance
to be ridiculous.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:47.834
Here's your chance to yell
"No! Let me drive the bus!"

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:50.534
-Yeah. Yes!
-And being -- and being ashamed

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:53.734
is a learned disease, right?
-Yeah.

00:03:53.734 --> 00:03:57.467
-Millions of teenagers are dying
of shame every day, right?

00:03:57.467 --> 00:03:59.300
-Yeah, I'm one of them. Yeah.
-Embarrassment.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.734
Embarrassed.
-Of course.

00:04:00.734 --> 00:04:04.334
-So what I'm hoping to do
is bring that moment together.

00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:07.267
It's not just about the kids,
but it's about the kids

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.701
seeing the parents
or the grown-ups in their lives

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:12.834
being silly, which allows them
to be more silly,

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.267
which allows the kids

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:16.834
to feel that
their silliness has validity.

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:19.234
-Yeah, and you can be silly.
It's fine to be silly.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:20.801
-Absolutely.
-It's fun to be silly.

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:24.434
-Absolutely.
-Does it blow your brains

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:28.167
to think that you're teaching
so many kids how to read?

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.968
Like, when parents
come up to you and go,

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:32.067
"You taught my kid how to read."
I mean, that's --

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:34.834
My kids learned on your books.
-That's very, very sweet.

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:40.100
But I think that I really only
put 49% of the book on the page.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.133
It is the grown-ups and the kids
and the way they strive

00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:46.033
and they create that makes
these things special.

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:49.534
And I want to make sure that
I create enough space for them

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:51.968
to make the stories
part of themselves, right?

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:53.934
-Wow. Yes, they are --
I totally agree.

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:55.601
And you do it every time.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:04:55.601 --> 00:04:57.734
-It is so --
-Thank you.

00:04:57.734 --> 00:05:01.367
-It's so clever.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:07.634
Your books are simple in the
best way, but so clever, also.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:10.067
-Well, simple and easy
are opposites.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:11.200
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.234
So, writing a picture book
is as easy

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:17.901
as describing the history
of Byzantium in three words.

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:19.634
-Yeah, you --

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:22.334
Yeah. Probably can't do it.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:25.000
But it's brilliantly done.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.801
And I was asking you
about this last time.

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:31.501
We were talking about
how you draw the pigeon.

00:05:31.501 --> 00:05:33.567
And you have an idea
There's something fun

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:36.901
you wanted us to try tonight.
-Yeah. So, look, I love drawing,

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:38.801
and this is why I love drawing.

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:41.133
For everyone,
not just for artists.

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:42.534
But, like,
when the band is playing,

00:05:42.534 --> 00:05:45.434
music is physicalized emotion.

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:49.000
Their body is creating emotion,
and it comes out as music.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.334
Well, drawing
is physicalized empathy.

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.601
When you are drawing something,

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.267
you are thinking
about that thing,

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:58.033
and you are understanding
that thing deeper.

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:00.734
So I make sure that
every one of my characters

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:03.133
is easy enough
for a 5-year-old to draw.

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:05.167
I think you have the drawing
ability of a 5-year-old.

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:07.300
-Thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.868
-I think the band has the
drawing ability of a 5-year-old.

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.267
-Yeah, there you go.
-Of several 5-year-olds.

00:06:11.267 --> 00:06:14.567
And I think you,
the audience, as well, right?

00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:17.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.067 --> 00:06:19.467
-Everyone,
look under your seats.

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:23.601
Everyone has a clipboard
and a marker.

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:26.067
Mo, you've drawn the pigeon
thousands of times.

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:28.033
-Yes.
-Can you teach 200 people here

00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:29.300
how to draw a pigeon?

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.534
-I'm gonna try and teach
200 people how to draw a pigeon.

00:06:32.534 --> 00:06:35.400
All right, here we go.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.467
-You're about to draw a cartoon!

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.701
All you have to know
to draw a cartoon

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.133
is how to write your name.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:43.701
Because your name
is a bunch of letters

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:46.033
put together
in the right order.

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:49.133
And a cartoon is
a bunch of shapes

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:53.000
put together in the right order.
I'm gonna make it easier.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.367
We're gonna draw
the pigeon's head.

00:06:54.367 --> 00:06:57.334
You only have to know
how to write my name,

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:59.767
Mo, and you will draw
the pigeon's head.

00:06:59.767 --> 00:07:01.200
So we're gonna do
a call-and-repeat, right?

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.100
Call and response. Ready?
-Yep.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.200
-You guys got your pens?
-I see 'em.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.734
Got your paper?

00:07:06.734 --> 00:07:09.501
All right.
From the O, here we go.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:10.901
Repeat after me.

00:07:10.901 --> 00:07:15.000
Big O.
-Big O!

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.501
-Middle O.
-Middle O!

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:22.934
-Little O!
-Little O!

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:27.100
-Darken it in!
-Darken it in!

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:31.300
-M hat.
-M hat.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.567
-Got it?

00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:35.534
Put it's sideways.
-There you go!

00:07:35.534 --> 00:07:37.934
-That's pigeon right there!
Come on!

00:07:37.934 --> 00:07:39.968
Audience, let me see it!
-Hold 'em up!

00:07:39.968 --> 00:07:42.601
-Let me see your pigeon!
-Look at that!

00:07:42.601 --> 00:07:44.567
Look at that!
[ Dinging ]

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:45.767
-That's fantastic.

00:07:45.767 --> 00:07:47.334
Look at that.
Isn't that amazing?

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.400
Mo Willems, everybody!
That's how you do it.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:50.567
-You guys are great!

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:52.467
-Both of his books
are available now.

00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:54.300
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show"!

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.801
Stick around.

